Build **Kessler**, an orbital demolition arcade game that runs entirely in the
browser.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Build the game there.

## Your starting point

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A TypeScript project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`, and its `README.md`
describes it. Its dependencies are installed, so you can build and test right
away: Vite builds it, Vitest tests it, ESLint lints it, and Prettier formats it.
That toolchain is the whole of what it supplies.

What is missing is everything under `src/`, and that directory does not exist
yet. This build runs on no engine, so the layer beneath the game is yours as
well as the game itself.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
A TypeScript project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`, and its `README.md`
describes it. Its dependencies are installed, so you can build and test right
away. It is wired up end to end: Vite builds it, Vitest tests it, ESLint lints
it, Prettier formats it, and `src/main.ts` already stands the engine up over the
page's canvas and runs the game.

What is missing is the game itself: `src/game.ts`. As seeded it is a stub whose
`initialize`, `update`, and `render` throw `"not implemented"`, and whose
`BACKGROUND` is a placeholder. The stub is written against the `KesslerState`
and `KesslerDebugApi` types the specification asks `src/game.ts` to declare, so
the project does not type-check until those declarations exist.

## The {{engine.name}} engine

This build runs on the **{{engine.name}}** engine, installed as a dependency of
the project, so import it by its bare package name; there is nothing to add to
`package.json` for it and nothing to install.

The engine's own documentation is at `{{engine.docs}}`. It is the authoritative
description of the engine's API; the specification in `specs/` describes the
game and does not restate the engine. The documentation also carries a complete
worked example of unit-testing a game built on it.
